40,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The records reveal a poignant truth: while we have an abundance of those who can model the future, the trained hands required for the present have largely departed for industry. We must be mindful of this void.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Midori,,"My findings are a bit concerning. While some subsidies are still there, shipping costs are rising while travel budgets are frozen. It seems the actual purchasing power of these awards is shrinking in some areas.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Wow, I found some really important stuff! The animal facility is doing great, but the glasshouses are totally full! Also, the spectroscopy suite was broken for eleven weeks because of a contract problem. That's a long time!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the ghosts of bureaucracy have been exorcised in one corner, as the repository turnaround is now remarkably swift. However, the portfolio review suggests a more stubborn demon: biological dependencies, specifically breeding colonies, are frequent catalysts for delay.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data confirms a recurring failure in our instincts. Mechanism-focused projects often leave their central questions unresolved, while catalogue-style awards provide lasting value. Furthermore, biological dependencies, like breeding colonies, are a primary source of schedule overruns.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, @Toshio, your observations on biological dependencies are most illuminating. It seems the land and the living are often less predictable than the numbers we use to describe them. We must seek a path of stability.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Kenji, that's a long time for a suite to be broken! It makes me worry that if the funding doesn't cover these rising costs, researchers might struggle even more when equipment fails or shipping gets expensive.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, exactly! It's scary to think about. If one engineer has to cover three different institutes, what happens if something else breaks? We can't pick a project that needs tools that are always busy or broken!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, your anxiety is well-founded. We are essentially gambling on the hope that the machinery of science remains operational. When combined with the biological volatility @Toshio and I noted, we risk funding a project that spends more time in waiting rooms than in laboratories.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, the risk is not merely a gamble, but a pattern. We have a history of favoring high-promise mechanisms that stall, while the plainer, resource-driven projects continue to deliver. We should prioritize reliability over ambition.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Toshio, your call for reliability is a wise one. However, we must remember that reliability is not just about the nature of the project, but about the people. A plain project will still fail if there are no trained hands to guide it.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Servas, I agree that the people are the heart of the research. But I'm also worried that if the budget doesn't keep up with real costs, even the most talented people will be too stressed to succeed.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, you're so right! It's like trying to build a cool Lego set but missing the most important pieces. If the tools are broken and the money is gone, everyone will just be sad and stressed!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, your Lego analogy is delightfully apt. We are indeed attempting to construct a masterpiece while the pieces are missing and the instructions are written in a language no one speaks. It seems we are all describing a systemic fragility.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, fragility is a choice we make when we prioritize the ""masterpiece"" over the foundation. If we continue to fund projects based on the allure of a breakthrough rather than the probability of delivery, we are simply repeating our past mistakes.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Servas,,It is a delicate balance we strike. Let us remember that the most brilliant vision is but a dream without the hands to realize it. I believe we must seek a candidate whose needs align with the strength of our current people.,"{""words"":42,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree, @Servas. We need a project that is realistic. If we can find a candidate that doesn't rely on the most expensive shipping or the most crowded facilities, it will be much easier for them to succeed.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I totally agree! We should look for a project that doesn't need the glasshouses since they're so full, and one that doesn't rely on that spectroscopy suite. Let's find a plan that actually fits what we have!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, your enthusiasm for a ""perfect fit"" is quite charming. Indeed, if we can avoid the biological volatility of breeding colonies and the logistical nightmares of broken machinery, we might actually see a project reach its conclusion before the next decade. I am ready to see which candidates are brave enough to be simple.","{""words"":54,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. Simplicity is not a lack of ambition, but a commitment to results. I will be looking for a candidate that avoids biological dependencies and focuses on delivering a tangible resource rather than an elusive mechanism.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
